<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Suakin</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Suakin is a port town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, built largely on a small coral island. A medieval trading port and a station for pilgrims bound for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, it came under Ottoman rule in the 16th century as part of the province of Habesh and was transferred to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1865. It declined after the British built Port Sudan nearby in the early 20th century, and its coral-stone buildings now lie in ruins.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suakin.jpg" alt="Suakin" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bertramz · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suakin,el-Geyf_mosque.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Suakin was probably the Limen Evangelis of Ptolemy, a port on a circular island at the end of a long inlet. Al-Hamdani first mentioned it by name in the 10th century and said it was already an ancient town. It was then a small Beja settlement that grew after the port of Badi to its south was abandoned. Venetian merchants were living there by the 14th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> was angered by one ruler, Ala al-Din al-Asba'ani, who seized the goods of merchants who died at sea nearby, and in 1264 a Mamluk force attacked and drove him out. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> records that in 1332 Suakin had a Muslim sultan, al-Sharif Zaid ibn-Abi Numayy ibn-'Ajlan, the son of a Meccan sharif.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> power declined in the late 14th century the Hedareb tribe took the port as their capital, and Suakin became the most important port on the north-east African Red Sea coast. The Portuguese besieged it in 1513 and captured it briefly in 1541. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-conquest-of-egypt">Ottoman conquest of Egypt</a> in 1517, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ozdemir-pasha">Özdemir Pasha</a> occupied Suakin in the early 1550s, and the province of Habesh was established in 1555 with its pasha residing there. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> restored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shafii-school">Shafi'i</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi</a> mosques and strengthened the fort. Trade nevertheless drifted away as the sea route around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> opened, and by the early 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-ludwig-burckhardt">Johann Ludwig Burckhardt</a> found two-thirds of the houses in ruins.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Khedive Isma'il received Suakin from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1865 and built houses, mills, mosques, hospitals and a church for immigrant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copts">Copts</a>. British forces were at Suakin in 1883-1885, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-kitchener">Lord Kitchener</a> made it his headquarters. After the defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdist-state">Mahdist State</a> the British chose to develop the new Port Sudan, about 50 kilometres north, and by 1922 the last of them had left.</p><p>The old city was built of coral taken from the sea bed, in houses up to four storeys high with white plaster, large casement windows and doors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> teak. Because the walls had no protective covering they decayed quickly, and the old city is now in ruins. In 2018 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> was granted a 99-year lease over Suakin island to restore the Ottoman port. The 2008 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 82,150 people in Suakin.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Suakin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Routes ran through the Bayuda Desert to Makuria, from Berber to the Red Sea ports of Badi, <b>Suakin</b> and Dahlak, and, Benjamin of Tudela wrote, west to Zuwila in Fezzan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alodia">Alodia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…with Ethiopia, ivory, civet, ostrich feathers and, above all, enslaved people captured in raids on the peoples of the southern and western frontiers were sent north to Egypt by caravan or east to the Red Sea port of <b>Suakin</b>, in exchange for cloth, metal goods, weapons and luxuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/funj-sultanate">Funj Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>He hoped to conquer the Ethiopian highlands, and the province's seat moved between Massawa, <b>Suakin</b> and later Jeddah.</blockquote><a href="/state/habesh-eyalet">Habesh Eyalet</a></li></ul></section>
